# Change pipeline: build directly, self-check, and only fan out when asked

**Do the work directly.** Changes to a playbook, role, inventory or data model
under `ansible-automation/` are made in this session, not handed to subagents.
Spawning a build/README/guide/review fan-out for every edit slows development
down more than it protects accuracy, so it is **opt-in**.

**Launch the documentation and review agents only when the user asks for
them**, in words like "run the pipeline", "get the docs agents on this" or
"have it reviewed". Absent that, do not launch them, do not announce that you
are about to, and do not treat a large change as implicit permission.

This is a change of *who does the work*, not of *what the work is*. The
obligations in `dev-workflow.mdc` still stand in full: a code change updates
the README(s) **and** the release note in the same commit, a rename needs a
repo-wide grep of the old name, and nothing commits until the user asks. Write
those updates yourself as part of the change.

## Self-check list, to run yourself on every change

This is the old review wave, reduced to what you can do directly in a minute.
It is the part whose absence actually costs something.

- Every filename, stage number, playbook name, variable and CLI flag named in
  the docs exists in the code, and every renamed identifier is gone
  repo-wide: `rg -n '<old-name>'`
- Commands are runnable as written from the stated working directory. In the
  Ansible collections that means the directory holding `ansible.cfg`, not
  `playbooks/`.
- README and guide agree with each other. They describe the same pipeline to
  two audiences; a contradiction is a bug in one of them.
- Playbooks still parse: `ansible-playbook --syntax-check` on each one. The DC
  and campus collections resolve their modules only in the script server's
  `~/venv`, so a clean check there is worth more than one here.
- Markdown anchor links resolve. GitHub lowercases a heading, drops
  punctuation and hyphenates spaces — an em dash in a heading yields a
  double hyphen and a dead link.
- `docs/index.html` tag balance is unchanged, since it is hand-edited HTML:

  ```bash
  python3 -c "
  import re; s=open('docs/index.html').read()
  for t in ['div','pre','ul','li','p','tr','td','table']:
      o,c=len(re.findall(r'<%s\b'%t,s)),len(re.findall(r'</%s>'%t,s))
      print(t,o,c,'OK' if o==c else 'MISMATCH')"
  ```

- Everything under `ansible-automation/02_data_center/` is pure ASCII. Check
  it with `rg -n '[^\x00-\x7F]' <path>` or a Python byte scan. **Never with
  BSD `grep`**: macOS grep does not interpret `\x` escapes, so
  `grep '[^\x00-\x7F]'` parses as a negated set of the literal characters
  `\ x 0 - 7 F`, matches nearly every line of prose, and can never fail. It
  has already produced one false green in this repo.
- A markdown line must not *begin* with `- ` unless it is meant to be a
  bullet. Rewrapping em-dash prose into ASCII hyphens is how that happens.

## When the user does ask for the pipeline

Four `generalPurpose` subagents in three waves, with these file boundaries.

| Wave | Agent | Owns these files, and nothing else |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Build** | `ansible-automation/**` except `**/README.md` |
| 2 (parallel) | **README** | `**/README.md` **and** `release-notes/YYYY-MM-DD.md` |
| 2 (parallel) | **Guide** | `docs/index.html` |
| 3 | **Review** | may edit any file in waves 1–2 to correct an error |

Wave 2 launches only after wave 1 is **verified** done. A truncated or
mid-sentence agent report is not completion — the agent is still running, and
launching wave 2 on it puts two agents on the same file. Confirm against the
tree (`git status`, the renamed paths, a syntax check) before proceeding.
Both wave-2 agents launch in the **same** tool block so they run
concurrently. Wave 3 launches only after both have returned.

### Rules that make the parallelism safe

- **The release note belongs to the README agent alone.** It is one file per
  date with bullets appended, so two agents writing it would clobber each
  other. The Guide agent must not touch `release-notes/`.
- **Never give two agents the same path.** The table above is the whole
  contract; if a change needs a file not listed, assign it explicitly in the
  prompt rather than letting two agents infer ownership.
- Nothing commits. Commit and push only when the user asks (see
  `dev-workflow.mdc`).
- "No change needed" is a legitimate result for a documentation agent. Say so
  in the prompt, or it will manufacture an edit to look busy.

### Briefing each agent

Subagents do not share this session's context. Every prompt must carry:

1. The repo path and the exact files wave 1 changed, with a one-line
   *what and why* per file.
2. The user's original request, verbatim — not a paraphrase.
3. The relevant constraints from `dev-workflow.mdc`, quoted, not referenced.
   A subagent cannot see that this is a dCloud lab, that credentials come
   only from the vault, or that `docs/index.html` is served by GitHub Pages.
4. Its file ownership from the table, stated as a hard boundary.
5. Any live evidence already in hand — a captured API payload, a `PLAY RECAP`,
   an exact error string. It turns a discovery task into an implementation
   task and is the difference between a useful agent and a slow one.
